welcome back to peterborough center the  british government has rejected claims  that shin feins election victory in  northern ireland could lead to the  breakup of the uk despite the party's  push a referendum on a united ireland  prime minister boris johnson is now  threatening to tear up post-brexit  trading arrangements with the eu despite  warnings from american president biden  that it could undermine the good friday  agreements are we edging ever closer to  a united ireland and a disunited united  kingdom i'm joined by someone who i'm  sure will have a strong view about this  the former irish prime minister and one  of the founding fathers of the good  friday agreement bertie hermes thank you  so much for joining me  how are you doing person very well i'm  going to say i'm i'm sort of twitchy  about what is going on politically with  the united kingdom with ireland with all  of it actually and i've been like this  since brexit and we've severed our way  from the european union it feels like a  lot of things have are being splinted do  you think we're going to see  a united island  looming or certainly a referendum on it  in the next few years and if we do  could this signal the beginning of the  end of the uk  well i think there are a number of  problems at the moment um  the elections last week were fairly good  i mean there were peaceful elections uh  most of the  political debate was on bread and butter  economic issues um except maybe for one  party the protocol was pushed a fair  loss but um there wasn't much about the  border pole or united ireland new  ireland shared ireland for different  names but people put on it so it wasn't  a big issue in the election and i  suppose  it is into good friday agreements that  from time to time there can be a  referendum and my view is that until the  preparatory work is done of how it would  look what it would cost how it would  evolve and that is not a good idea to  have it and i think most people are of  that view so it will there will be a  vote at some stage but the it's early  stages before the work is done but  unfortunately  that has moved away from the issue a  little bit what happens now  um is that brexit is back into the game  again and the northern ireland protocol  which was part of the withdrawal  agreement that prime minister boris used  to say brexit was done  that  now the british government through the  attorney general through various  ministers are saying that they might  unilaterally  bring in legislation to amend that that  has created i think great friction on  the whole island of ireland uh the  government here are uh really upset  about it i think our american colleagues  are very upset about it uh president  biden is but well it's a betrayal  it's a betrayal of the agreement and the  bottom line with all this is that when  brexit was getting done as boris johnson  kept telling us nobody had worked out  what to do about the border situation  and that has never been properly  resolved and that's why he's trying to  reopen it now because he knows he didn't  have a plan for it and it's not worked  in any way that suits anybody and that  seems to me the fundamental problem  right from the start they never sorted  this before during or after brexit  well we're still i think um  very study even after brexit when the  debate took place under withdrawal  agreement and  not to make sure we've no land border uh  on the island of ireland uh boris  johnson negotiated with the irish  government and with the european union  uh that protocol and and within a few  months of them negotiating it he said  that he didn't like it and it it was it  was a bad idea but he was the one who  negotiated it but right you know i think  what i'd like to say first is that this  thing is negotiated and sorted out in a  namical way if the british government go  down the road  of arbitrarily and breaking the with the  protocol there will be a response  and from from europe  they will probably start looking at  sections of the trade deal and it'll  it'll just make a total mess of  everything rather than have a negotiated  negotiated settlement and then you know  several ministers in the last 24 hour  secretaries of state including the  attorney general seem to be indicating  and they're not saying that definitively  but they seem to be indicating that the  british government  are prepared to unilaterally change the  protocol without consultation with the  irish government or europe and that  would just be an absolute mess i think  it would be a complete fiasco and  another betrayal of what was agreed let  me i don't know if you're a betting man  uh mr herm but if you were a betting man  or a soothsayer whatever you want to put  it nostradamus for a moment just go  forward 20 years how feasible could it  be  that there's by then a united ireland uh  the scotland has gone independent that  the uk  basically is left with england and wales  i mean is that something which actually  right now could be a realistic uh  situation in 20 years time  in 20 years time i think it could be i  think scotland will come up first again  uh particularly if scottish nationalist  party go in with labor after the next  election if they were to have enough  seats to form a government that's a big  if but it is a possibility  there will be um in that time scale uh  hopefully the work will be completed and  there will be a border poll it'll be up  to the people what they decide um but um  i think you know  from my point of view it should be on  the basis of consent and agreement the  one thing we don't need on the island of  ireland  is to go back into the troubled days so  it will be the ability of the  politicians over the next 20 years to  see if they can both do the preparatory  work and be able to negotiate and be  able to satisfy most of the unionists i  i don't think you'll ever get everybody  on board and nobody probably should  expect that but it it will be a  challenge but  i think what's what's more difficult  persons but  if if we can't resolve uh even the  protocol how will we ever resolve the  bigger issues that are down the road and  and this is really and the protocol by  the way i should say for your  your viewers is really what was brought  in negotiated between  europe and and boris johnson uh to avoid  a border a land border in ireland uh so  that we could um  northern ireland would stay in the  single market yeah and be able to get  investment be able to get jobs if he now  rips it up frankly it's another breach  of trust and that's the problem and i  think that that that in its way i think  the way boris johnson is going about  this is going to break trust so badly i  think it makes it more not less likely  that the uk ultimately fractures because  why would anyone trust him uh i've got  to leave it there i'm afraid uh bernier  brilliant to talk to you thank you so  much for coming on piers morgan and  simpson really appreciate it  thank you very much  thank you

